1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,023
Your car breaks down on Monday.

2
00:00:03,023 --> 00:00:05,489
Your phone screen cracks on Tuesday.

3
00:00:05,489 --> 00:00:07,817
And on Wednesday, the rent is due.

4
00:00:08,077 --> 00:00:10,682
If one surprise bill can wreck your month,

5
00:00:10,682 --> 00:00:12,791
you don't have a spending problem.

6
00:00:12,791 --> 00:00:14,465
You have a cushion problem.

7
00:00:14,725 --> 00:00:15,663
In this video,

8
00:00:15,663 --> 00:00:17,872
you'll learn exactly how big your

9
00:00:17,872 --> 00:00:19,546
emergency fund should be,

10
00:00:19,546 --> 00:00:22,090
where to keep it, and how to build it,

11
00:00:22,090 --> 00:00:23,630
even on a tight budget.

12
00:00:25,030 --> 00:00:27,081
Welcome back to Broke to Balance.

13
00:00:27,081 --> 00:00:28,386
I'm glad you're here.

14
00:00:28,646 --> 00:00:30,320
Let's build your safety net.

15
00:00:30,580 --> 00:00:31,367
Quick note:

16
00:00:31,367 --> 00:00:32,798
this is educational,

17
00:00:32,798 --> 00:00:34,944
not personal financial advice.

18
00:00:34,944 --> 00:00:36,231
Let's get into it.

19
00:00:37,631 --> 00:00:39,784
An emergency fund is money set

20
00:00:39,784 --> 00:00:41,578
aside for true surprises,

21
00:00:41,578 --> 00:00:42,798
and nothing else.

22
00:00:43,058 --> 00:00:44,894
It's not a vacation fund.

23
00:00:44,894 --> 00:00:46,803
It's not a new phone fund.

24
00:00:46,803 --> 00:00:49,594
It's your protection against job loss,

25
00:00:49,594 --> 00:00:50,622
medical bills,

26
00:00:50,622 --> 00:00:52,899
and urgent car or home repairs.

27
00:00:53,159 --> 00:00:53,976
Without one,

28
00:00:53,976 --> 00:00:56,565
a surprise bill goes on a credit card.

29
00:00:56,565 --> 00:00:58,813
And that's how a one-time problem

30
00:00:58,813 --> 00:01:00,857
turns into months of interest.

31
00:01:01,117 --> 00:01:01,734
With one,

32
00:01:01,734 --> 00:01:04,547
a surprise is just an annoying afternoon.

33
00:01:05,947 --> 00:01:08,883
Let's make this real with two people facing the

34
00:01:08,883 --> 00:01:11,069
same eight hundred dollar surprise.

35
00:01:11,329 --> 00:01:13,837
Alex has no emergency fund,

36
00:01:13,837 --> 00:01:18,016
so the bill goes on a credit card at 24% APR.

37
00:01:18,276 --> 00:01:20,197
Paying fifty dollars a month,

38
00:01:20,197 --> 00:01:22,714
it takes about twenty months to clear,

39
00:01:22,714 --> 00:01:24,635
and costs roughly one hundred

40
00:01:24,635 --> 00:01:26,821
seventy-four dollars in interest.

41
00:01:27,081 --> 00:01:28,721
Sam has a starter fund.

42
00:01:28,721 --> 00:01:30,931
Sam pays the bill from savings,

43
00:01:30,931 --> 00:01:33,641
then pauses wants spending and refills

44
00:01:33,641 --> 00:01:35,780
the account within two months,

45
00:01:35,780 --> 00:01:37,492
with no interest at all.

46
00:01:37,752 --> 00:01:40,600
Same surprise, completely different outcome.

47
00:01:40,600 --> 00:01:42,607
That's what a cushion buys you:

48
00:01:42,607 --> 00:01:43,967
not wealth, but calm.

49
00:01:45,367 --> 00:01:48,169
Step one is a starter emergency fund.

50
00:01:48,429 --> 00:01:50,889
A common goal is five hundred to

51
00:01:50,889 --> 00:01:52,503
one thousand dollars.

52
00:01:52,503 --> 00:01:55,270
That covers many everyday surprises,

53
00:01:55,270 --> 00:01:58,421
like a tire, a copay, or a broken laptop.

54
00:01:58,681 --> 00:02:00,862
Start here before anything else,

55
00:02:00,862 --> 00:02:03,247
even before aggressive debt payoff,

56
00:02:03,247 --> 00:02:06,041
because it stops new debt from piling up.

57
00:02:06,301 --> 00:02:08,568
At six hundred dollars a month from

58
00:02:08,568 --> 00:02:10,317
your twenty percent bucket,

59
00:02:10,317 --> 00:02:12,519
a one thousand dollar starter fund

60
00:02:12,519 --> 00:02:14,267
takes less than two months.

61
00:02:15,667 --> 00:02:18,235
Step two is a full emergency fund:

62
00:02:18,235 --> 00:02:21,406
three to six months of essential expenses.

63
00:02:21,666 --> 00:02:23,367
Notice the word essential.

64
00:02:23,367 --> 00:02:25,526
We're counting only the bills you

65
00:02:25,526 --> 00:02:27,096
must pay to stay housed,

66
00:02:27,096 --> 00:02:28,208
fed, and working.

67
00:02:28,468 --> 00:02:31,144
Let's use our example from the fifty,

68
00:02:31,144 --> 00:02:32,663
thirty, twenty video.

69
00:02:32,663 --> 00:02:34,616
Rent, one thousand dollars.

70
00:02:34,616 --> 00:02:36,135
Groceries, two fifty.

71
00:02:36,135 --> 00:02:37,799
Utilities, one hundred.

72
00:02:37,799 --> 00:02:39,824
Transportation, one hundred.

73
00:02:39,824 --> 00:02:42,573
Insurance and minimum payments, fifty.

74
00:02:42,833 --> 00:02:45,073
That's fifteen hundred dollars a month.

75
00:02:45,333 --> 00:02:48,409
Three months is forty-five hundred dollars.

76
00:02:48,409 --> 00:02:50,412
Six months is nine thousand.

77
00:02:51,812 --> 00:02:54,120
So should you aim for three months or six,

78
00:02:54,120 --> 00:02:54,560
or more?

79
00:02:54,820 --> 00:02:58,091
Lean toward three months if you have stable income,

80
00:02:58,091 --> 00:03:00,400
a second earner, and few dependents.

81
00:03:00,660 --> 00:03:02,993
Lean toward six months or more if

82
00:03:02,993 --> 00:03:04,760
your income is irregular,

83
00:03:04,760 --> 00:03:06,244
you're self-employed,

84
00:03:06,244 --> 00:03:07,869
you're the only earner,

85
00:03:07,869 --> 00:03:10,343
or you work in a volatile industry.

86
00:03:10,603 --> 00:03:12,290
There's no perfect number.

87
00:03:12,290 --> 00:03:14,691
Pick a target you can actually reach,

88
00:03:14,691 --> 00:03:16,963
and adjust it as your life changes.

89
00:03:18,363 --> 00:03:20,029
Where should you keep this money?

90
00:03:20,289 --> 00:03:23,070
Somewhere safe, separate, and easy to reach,

91
00:03:23,070 --> 00:03:24,144
but not too easy.

92
00:03:24,404 --> 00:03:27,570
A high-yield savings account at an insured bank

93
00:03:27,570 --> 00:03:29,994
or credit union is a popular choice.

94
00:03:29,994 --> 00:03:32,216
It's separate from your checking,

95
00:03:32,216 --> 00:03:34,506
so you won't spend it by accident,

96
00:03:34,506 --> 00:03:36,728
and it usually pays more interest

97
00:03:36,728 --> 00:03:38,344
than a standard account.

98
00:03:38,604 --> 00:03:42,041
Check that deposits are insured where you live.

99
00:03:42,041 --> 00:03:42,918
In the U.S.,

100
00:03:42,918 --> 00:03:45,331
the FDIC and NCUA typically cover

101
00:03:45,331 --> 00:03:47,671
up to two hundred fifty thousand

102
00:03:47,671 --> 00:03:49,279
dollars per depositor,

103
00:03:49,279 --> 00:03:52,204
per institution, per ownership category.

104
00:03:52,464 --> 00:03:55,130
Rates change, so compare current offers,

105
00:03:55,130 --> 00:03:57,530
and skip accounts with monthly fees.

106
00:03:57,790 --> 00:03:59,973
What you shouldn't do is keep your

107
00:03:59,973 --> 00:04:02,220
emergency fund in the stock market,

108
00:04:02,220 --> 00:04:05,430
or in something you can't reach within a few days.

109
00:04:05,430 --> 00:04:08,383
Emergencies don't wait for markets to recover.

110
00:04:09,783 --> 00:04:11,300
Now, how do you build it?

111
00:04:11,560 --> 00:04:11,986
First,

112
00:04:11,986 --> 00:04:15,609
open a separate account and name it Emergency Fund.

113
00:04:15,609 --> 00:04:17,810
A name makes it harder to raid.

114
00:04:18,070 --> 00:04:19,393
Second, automate it.

115
00:04:19,393 --> 00:04:21,113
Set a transfer for payday,

116
00:04:21,113 --> 00:04:23,494
just like the pay-yourself-first tip

117
00:04:23,494 --> 00:04:25,147
from our budgeting video.

118
00:04:25,407 --> 00:04:27,779
Third, feed it with extra money:

119
00:04:27,779 --> 00:04:30,298
tax refunds, bonuses, side income,

120
00:04:30,298 --> 00:04:33,633
or the savings from a cancelled subscription.

121
00:04:33,893 --> 00:04:36,272
Here's the timeline if you save six

122
00:04:36,272 --> 00:04:37,903
hundred dollars a month,

123
00:04:37,903 --> 00:04:39,263
before any interest.

124
00:04:39,263 --> 00:04:41,914
The starter fund is done in two months.

125
00:04:41,914 --> 00:04:44,633
A three-month fund of forty-five hundred

126
00:04:44,633 --> 00:04:46,876
dollars takes about eight months.

127
00:04:46,876 --> 00:04:49,255
And the full six-month fund of nine

128
00:04:49,255 --> 00:04:51,838
thousand dollars takes fifteen months.

129
00:04:53,238 --> 00:04:55,714
Once it exists, you need a few rules.

130
00:04:55,974 --> 00:04:56,605
Rule one:

131
00:04:56,605 --> 00:04:59,340
ask whether it's unexpected, necessary,

132
00:04:59,340 --> 00:05:00,111
and urgent.

133
00:05:00,111 --> 00:05:01,654
If it isn't all three,

134
00:05:01,654 --> 00:05:03,197
it isn't an emergency.

135
00:05:03,457 --> 00:05:04,041
Rule two:

136
00:05:04,041 --> 00:05:06,182
if you use it, don't feel guilty.

137
00:05:06,182 --> 00:05:08,064
That's exactly what it's for.

138
00:05:08,324 --> 00:05:09,049
Rule three:

139
00:05:09,049 --> 00:05:09,708
refill it.

140
00:05:09,708 --> 00:05:12,674
Pause your wants spending for a month or two,

141
00:05:12,674 --> 00:05:13,465
and rebuild.

142
00:05:14,865 --> 00:05:16,139
People always ask:

143
00:05:16,139 --> 00:05:18,121
should I pay off debt first,

144
00:05:18,121 --> 00:05:20,103
or build the emergency fund?

145
00:05:20,363 --> 00:05:22,700
A popular approach is to build the

146
00:05:22,700 --> 00:05:24,418
small starter fund first,

147
00:05:24,418 --> 00:05:26,548
then attack high-interest debt,

148
00:05:26,548 --> 00:05:29,502
then grow your fund to three to six months.

149
00:05:29,762 --> 00:05:30,019
Why?

150
00:05:30,019 --> 00:05:32,006
Because with no cushion at all,

151
00:05:32,006 --> 00:05:35,019
the next surprise just lands on the card again,

152
00:05:35,019 --> 00:05:37,199
and you're back where you started.

153
00:05:37,459 --> 00:05:38,647
Another question:

154
00:05:38,647 --> 00:05:40,533
what about irregular costs,

155
00:05:40,533 --> 00:05:43,259
like annual insurance or holiday gifts?

156
00:05:43,259 --> 00:05:45,006
Those aren't emergencies,

157
00:05:45,006 --> 00:05:46,403
they're predictable.

158
00:05:46,403 --> 00:05:48,710
Give them their own sinking fund,

159
00:05:48,710 --> 00:05:50,806
a little set aside each month.

160
00:05:51,066 --> 00:05:52,868
And if you're self-employed,

161
00:05:52,868 --> 00:05:55,186
aim for the higher end of the range,

162
00:05:55,186 --> 00:05:57,117
because your income can swing.

163
00:05:58,517 --> 00:06:01,054
Before we wrap up, four common mistakes.

164
00:06:01,314 --> 00:06:01,566
One:

165
00:06:01,566 --> 00:06:04,391
leaving it in your everyday checking account,

166
00:06:04,391 --> 00:06:06,149
where it quietly gets spent.

167
00:06:06,409 --> 00:06:06,652
Two:

168
00:06:06,652 --> 00:06:08,653
investing it in the stock market,

169
00:06:08,653 --> 00:06:11,565
where it could be down exactly when you need it.

170
00:06:11,825 --> 00:06:12,190
Three:

171
00:06:12,190 --> 00:06:14,136
using it and never refilling it.

172
00:06:14,396 --> 00:06:15,020
And four:

173
00:06:15,020 --> 00:06:18,276
waiting for the perfect number before starting.

174
00:06:18,276 --> 00:06:21,186
Even fifty dollars a week is a real start.

175
00:06:22,586 --> 00:06:23,675
Let's recap.

176
00:06:23,935 --> 00:06:25,976
Start with a starter fund of five

177
00:06:25,976 --> 00:06:27,954
hundred to one thousand dollars.

178
00:06:28,214 --> 00:06:30,115
Grow it to three to six months

179
00:06:30,115 --> 00:06:31,509
of essential expenses.

180
00:06:31,769 --> 00:06:33,793
Keep it in a separate, insured,

181
00:06:33,793 --> 00:06:35,229
easy-to-reach account,

182
00:06:35,229 --> 00:06:36,992
and automate your deposits.

183
00:06:37,252 --> 00:06:38,128
And remember,

184
00:06:38,128 --> 00:06:41,632
even a small start beats waiting for a perfect plan.

185
00:06:41,892 --> 00:06:43,067
In the next video,

186
00:06:43,067 --> 00:06:45,937
we'll tackle the other side of the equation:

187
00:06:45,937 --> 00:06:48,416
how to stop overspending with a simple

188
00:06:48,416 --> 00:06:50,113
thirty-minute money audit.

189
00:06:50,373 --> 00:06:51,962
If this helped, subscribe,

190
00:06:51,962 --> 00:06:53,674
and tell me in the comments:

191
00:06:53,674 --> 00:06:55,630
what's your emergency fund goal?

192
00:06:55,890 --> 00:06:58,534
This video is for education only and

193
00:06:58,534 --> 00:07:00,222
isn't financial advice.

194
00:07:00,222 --> 00:07:01,544
See you next time.

